  "question": "We have very sparse arachnid data at Elephants by the Lake. Search public occurrences and research deposits in the declared dry-Deccan donor belt, nominate regional species only from retrieved evidence, then apply both an environmental-feature gate and a climate-envelope gate before calling any species expected at EBTL. Which candidates pass, which fail, and what should we survey next?",
  "elapsed_wall_s": 83.582,
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "One species, Thelacantha brevispina, is already observed at Elephants by the Lake (one record from GBIF, human observation). Three other species—Gasteracantha geminata, Plexippus petersi, and Hyllus semicupreus—were found in the donor belt but failed the environmental-feature gate (their occurrence records come from areas outside the AlphaEarth analog space). All four passed the climate-envelope gate. Because none of the non-observed candidates passed both gates, no transfer is admissible. To move forward, conduct a targeted arachnid survey at the site: sample along the lake fringe and adjacent forest edge using pitfall traps and visual searches, and repeat across seasons. Only new local detections can confirm presence for the other species.",
